# TESTING-HARNESS.md §2 — "`idle` is true exactly when … no in-flight app requests, running
# jobs, pending frame, live toast timer, or armed debounce."
#
# This is the scenario that would have caught three of those five counters being written by
# nothing: with `in_flight_requests`, `pending_frame` and `armed_debounces` structurally zero,
# `await idle` returned in under a millisecond on a cold start and every later line raced the
# work it was supposed to wait for. Each clause below reads one constituent *by name*, so a
# counter that stops being fed fails here rather than silently making every `await idle` a
# no-op.
#
# No screenshot: the whole subject is a JSON object, and this scenario is part of the slice
# that rides `make test` in the headless lane (§7).
fixture: one-repo
await idle 30000
assert idle.idle == true && idle.in_flight_requests == 0 && idle.running_jobs == 0
assert idle.pending_frame == false && idle.live_toast_timers == 0 && idle.armed_debounces == 0

# A real daemon job through the run's own client: `idle` is false while it runs, and the bare
# word `idle` and the counter it is derived from must never disagree.
job long
await idle.running_jobs > 0 20000
assert idle.idle == false
dump busy

# `job long` is stopped at teardown, so the run does not depend on it finishing. What it does
# have to show is the job reaching a terminal state in `jobs[]` once it is cancelled — which
# `daemon kill` would confound, so the accounting below uses a job that ends on its own.
job success
await jobs[1].status == succeeded 30000
assert screen == Hub && sticky_error absent

# A toast is the fourth counter, and it is the one the app's own clock owns: `advance` moves
# the stored dwell rather than sleeping it out (§1).
key y
await toasts[0] exists 15000
assert idle.live_toast_timers > 0 && idle.idle == false
advance 20000
await toasts[0] absent 15000
assert idle.live_toast_timers == 0
dump settled
quit
